Present as Chrome for Android, and let the extension stores install.

Absorbs, in this order: ohos-ua-chrome-android.patch, ohos-extension-store.patch, ohos-store-ua-tab-helper.patch. Their descriptions follow.

--- ohos-ua-chrome-android.patch

Stop telling tablets and PCs that they are Android.

Every HarmonyOS form factor sent an Android token. Sites read "Android
without Mobile" as an Android tablet, and Baidu, Taobao, JD, Bilibili and
Weibo answered a tablet or a 2in1 with the phone page. Ten large sites were
compared at 1280x800 with and without the token: nine switch to the desktop
page once it goes, and the tenth, qq.com, gives tablets the phone page
whatever the UA says.

So off the phone the User-Agent is HarmonyOS's own shape, which is what
ArkWeb sends on those devices -- the OpenHarmony and ArkWeb tokens carry the
platform rather than decorating an Android one:

  Mozilla/5.0 (Tablet; OpenHarmony 7.0) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko)
      Chrome/154.0.0.0 Safari/537.36 ArkWeb/7.0.0.105

The phone keeps the Chrome-on-Android string, and everything that has to agree
with it keeps agreeing: UA-CH platform Android 10.0.0, navigator.platform
Linux armv81, no PDF plugin. Those were not decoration either -- a UA mixing
OpenHarmony, Android and ArkWeb tokens with a platform matching none of them
read as a spoofed browser and Cloudflare Turnstile refused it with 600010.
Off the phone the same rule gives the opposite answers, so UA-CH says platform
HarmonyOS with HarmonyOS's own version, mobile ?0, and the desktop PDF viewer
is listed.

Phone or not is read from --use-mobile-user-agent rather than from the device
class, because the shell sets it from the UI family: an unfolded foldable is
not a phone, and a phone with "desktop site" turned on is not one either.
Both then get the tablet string, which is what those two settings are asking
for. A foldable's hardware class is "phone", so it would otherwise be labelled
Phone in a UA with no Mobile token -- Tablet is the honest label for a
tablet-sized window.

--- ohos-extension-store.patch

Let a tablet install an extension at all.

A tablet or a 2in1 got the mobile store, whose button is "add to home
screen" -- so an extension could not be installed at all, which is the
whole point of having the store.

The store reads the User-Agent string and nothing else. Four shapes were
compared on a tablet-sized window with the devtools protocol setting the UA
and the Client Hints together:

  PC; OpenHarmony 7.0; Android 10   mobile site, "add to home screen"
  PC; OpenHarmony 7.0               mobile site
  ... with UA-CH platform Linux     mobile site, so the hints are ignored
  PC; OpenHarmony 7.0; X11          desktop site, install button

One windowing token is the difference. It cannot go in the UA everyone
gets: with "X11; Linux" in it, qq.com serves its phone pages. So the store
gets it on the navigation, through the same per-entry override "desktop
site" uses, and every other site is untouched. Phones are left alone
entirely -- they have no extensions UI, and the desktop store would be
unusable on one.

The Client Hints stay as they are. The string still names OpenHarmony, so
platform HarmonyOS agrees with it; a UA-CH platform disagreeing with the UA
is exactly what read as a spoofed browser when this port last tried it.

Second, the update query said os=HarmonyOS. That string picks the package
the server sends, and there is no such platform there, so it answered
nothing: every CRX download and every extension update asked for a platform
that does not exist. It says linux now, which with the arm64 already beside
it is a combination the server serves, and an extension's CRX is the same
bytes on all of them.

--- ohos-store-ua-tab-helper.patch

Watch every tab for the web store, from the moment it exists.

The store user agent -- the desktop string with X11 in the platform
section, without which the store will not install anything -- did not
reach the store page on a 2in1: navigator.userAgent there was the
ordinary desktop "(PC; OpenHarmony 7.0)".

The watcher that installs it was attached from two places, both driven
by the shell: the state snapshot the shell polls, and the shell's own
"navigate" command. A navigation that neither of those saw coming --
typed into Chromium's own omnibox in the native UI, opened from a link,
restored, or started in a tab created between two snapshots -- reached
the store before any watcher was there to see it, and the watcher only
acts on DidStartNavigation.

TabHelpers::AttachTabHelpers is where Chromium attaches things every tab
should have, before that tab loads anything, whatever created it. The two
shell-driven attach points stay: creating the observer again is a no-op.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.
Apply with:

  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-ua-web-store.patch

